Short answer: yes for construction and classification, no for conversion. PH gives you a degree certificate and a rational-offset construction. It does not turn a quintic into a cubic. A cubic PH curve and a quintic PH curve are different families; the only “both” case is degree elevation, where the quintic is not a genuine PH quintic.

The degree bookkeeping. For a planar polynomial PH curve,
(x'(t), y'(t)) = (u(t)² − v(t)², 2 u(t) v(t))
degree n = 2 · max(deg u, deg v) + 1
- u, v linear → cubic PH;
- u, v quadratic → quintic PH;
- constant → line/point.

There is no proper PH family in between. The PH condition is what makes the arc length rational (σ = u² + v²) and the offset rational; it is independent of whether the Bézier span is cubic or quintic.

Why elevation is not a PH quintic. Every cubic Bézier can be written as a quintic by degree elevation. But the elevated curve’s derivative is still quadratic, so its PH factorization is still u, v linear. A PH-degree test reports cubic, not quintic. A genuine quintic PH curve has a quartic derivative; it cannot be a cubic Bézier unless its quadratic u, v degenerate to linear — and then it is the cubic PH family, not a new object. So the intersection of the proper cubic-PH and quintic-PH families is the cubic family (plus degenerate lines).
The two questions are separate:
question	test	what it tells you
Is the Bézier span cubic or quintic?	d4 = 0 and d5 = 0 on the control polygon	the representation degree — the side list uses this
Is the curve PH, and at what degree?	factor the hodograph into u² − v², 2uv; take max(deg u, deg v)	rational offset / rational arc length, and the PH degree
A cubic Bézier is not automatically PH. The integer-cubic family in curve_lab/pyth_cubic.py is the PH cubics; a generic G3 cubic fit is just a cubic Bézier. Likewise, the ph_quintic integer examples are genuine quintic PH curves; the catalogue’s fitted quintics are not PH in general.
What PH is genuinely good for here: finding structured curves with rational offsets and exact arc length — the stem/rail problem. Parameterize u, v (linear for cubic, quadratic for quintic), integrate the hodograph, and use Pythagorean triples for integer controls. That is what pyth_cubic and ph_quintic do. It reduces the search from arbitrary Bézier controls to a small Pythagorean parameter space.
Practical recommendation:
- To classify cubic vs quintic spans, keep using d4/d5 — it is exact, direct, and already on the cubic-native side list.
- To classify PH and get rational offsets, factor the hodograph; the PH degree is 2 max(deg u, deg v) + 1.
- Do not search for a separate “both cubic and quintic” family. The cubic is the minimal representation; the quintic is its elevation. A genuine quintic PH curve will not collapse to a cubic.
- If you want a curve that is both PH and cubic (for a rational-offset rail), use the cubic PH construction; if you want PH and quintic, use the quintic one. They are different curves, not two views of the same one — except for the cubic’s trivial quintic elevation, whose PH degree remains cubic.

