1. The Core Mathematical Property
For a standard 2D parametric curve \(\mathbf{r}(t) = (x(t), y(t))\), calculating its speed or arc length requires taking the square root of the sum of squared derivatives: \(\sqrt{x^{\prime }(t)^{2}+y^{\prime }(t)^{2}}\). Because of that square root, general curves rarely have exact, closed-form arc lengths or rational parallel offsets. [1, 2, 3]
