To understand PHH curves, it helps to look at their two parent curve families:
Pythagorean-Hodograph (PH) Curves. Introduced by Rida Farouki and Takis Sakkalis in 1990, a PH curve is a polynomial parametric curve whose derivative (hodograph) components satisfy a Pythagorean condition. In 2D, this means x'(t)² + y'(t)² = σ(t)² for some polynomial σ(t). Because the speed function σ(t) is a simple polynomial rather than a square root, PH curves allow for exact, closed-form arc length calculations and rational offset curves. [1, 2, 3]
H-Bézier Curves. These are generalized Bézier curves defined over a space of hyperbolic functions (such as  and ) rather than purely algebraic polynomials. They are widely used in Computer-Aided Geometric Design (CAGD) because they can represent transcendental curves—like catenaries and hyperbolas—precisely while offering shape parameters to adjust the curve without moving control points.
How PHH Curves Work
While H-Bézier curves offer great geometric flexibility, computing their arc lengths and parallel offsets requires difficult numerical approximations. Conversely, PH curves compute these properties exactly, but they are more restrictive to manipulate directly via control polygons. [1, 2]
Researchers developed PHH-Bézier curves to bridge this gap: [1, 2]
Geometric Construction. A cubic PHH-Bézier curve is constructed directly from the control polygon of a given cubic H-Bézier curve using geometric relationships (such as orderly triangles). [1]
Control Point Blending. To minimize the geometric error between the original H-Bézier curve and its PH approximation, algorithms blend the middle control points of the PH curve with those of the original H-Bézier curve. [1]
Spline Extensions. Beyond single curve segments, this method extends to PHH splines, allowing designers to stitch multiple segments together smoothly for complex paths. [1]
Key Applications
Because they preserve the mathematical benefits of standard Pythagorean hodograph curves while approximating hyperbolic shapes, PHH curves are primarily studied for use in: [1, 2]
CAD/CAGD Modeling. Modeling complex industrial surfaces and sweep paths that require both hyperbolic shape control and exact metric properties. [1, 2, 3, 4]
