 I was imprecise, and the number was one I had not derived. Here is the arithmetic, which is now also on the page (5ebddbd).
Where the family comes from. A PH quintic is built from w = a + bt + ct², a complex polynomial with six real numbers in it (three coefficients, each with a real and imaginary part). The curve is the integral of w².
Two of those six are not shape. Rotating the plane and scaling it amount to multiplying w by one complex number — two real numbers. They change where the curve points and how big it is, not what it looks like. So the shapes form a four-parameter family.
Fix the ends and you have two knobs left. Pin both endpoints: two conditions. Pin both tangent directions: two more. Four conditions out of six leaves two free numbers — the two speeds at the ends. That is exactly the parameter we called s0 and s2, and it is why PHH is a family rather than one curve, and why your √|Δ| rule is a choice among infinitely many.
So should there be more examples? No — not as a fix. Three examples and thirty examples are both just samples of a continuous family; the fourth parameter is invisible either way. What shows a family is a sweep: one coefficient stepped across nine values, drawn together. The interactive already does this by hand — the u/v sliders walk the family, and each position is a different member. If you want it as a static figure, say so and I'll add a Fig. 36 with a nine-member sweep.
